# ==================================================================================================
# /etc/logrotate.d/msms — Multi-School Management System
#
# SRS §27 "Logging". Checklist row 7.10, the OPERATING-SYSTEM half.
#
# §27 lists twelve deployment technologies and states that no technology beyond that list is
# introduced. "logrotate" is not one of them, so the sanction for this file is worth stating rather
# than leaving to be reconstructed: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md:215-216 names "a logrotate config" as one
# of the artifacts `deploy/` holds, and docs/IMPLEMENTATION_CHECKLIST.md:723 is the row this file
# completes — that row still reads "Deployment-side logrotate does not exist", which is stale as of
# this file landing on disk and is the line to update. logrotate is the operating system's
# implementation of §27's "Logging", the way certbot is the conventional means of obtaining §27's
# "SSL", not a thirteenth technology. Nothing here needs Docker, systemd units, or a metrics agent.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE ROTATES — five files, and not one of them is winston's
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js:213 pins `const PM2_LOG_DIR = '/var/log/msms'` and joins four
# filenames onto it — :420-421 for msms-api, :530-531 for msms-cron:
#
#     /var/log/msms/msms-api-out.log
#     /var/log/msms/msms-api-error.log
#     /var/log/msms/msms-cron-out.log
#     /var/log/msms/msms-cron-error.log
#
# plus one more that belongs to no other mechanism on this box and is the easiest of the five to
# forget: the PM2 daemon's own __PM2_HOME__/pm2.log, in stanza 3.
#
# The application half of §27's "Logging" already exists and this file must not interfere with it.
# backend/src/config/logger.js configures winston-daily-rotate-file, which rotates by date AND
# prunes by age on its own. Everything below is written around that fact, starting here.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY winston's FILES ARE NEVER NAMED, AND WHY NO STANZA USES A GLOB
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# winston writes to LOG_DIR. By default that is backend/storage/logs (env.js:242-244,
# .env.example:107, deploy/env/production.env.example:654) — a different directory from
# /var/log/msms, which the ecosystem file chose for exactly this separation (its comment at
# :190-201). The scheduler has a second directory, backend/storage/logs/cron, because
# ecosystem.config.js:502 sets LOG_DIR for that app alone, so that two resident processes do not
# fight over one combined-%DATE%.log and one shared audit ledger.
#
# A separate directory is the first line of defence and it is NOT sufficient on its own, because
# LOG_DIR may be absolute: env.js:242-243 uses the value verbatim when path.isAbsolute() is true,
# and deploy/monitoring/README.md:581-584 calls `LOG_DIR=/var/log/msms` "a perfectly normal
# production choice". Set that way, error-<date>.log and combined-<date>.log land in this file's
# directory, right beside PM2's four.
#
# That is why every stanza below names its files EXPLICITLY — not `*.log`, not `msms-*.log`, not a
# glob of any kind. Adding an app to the ecosystem file means adding its two filenames here by
# hand. That is deliberate friction, and it buys a second thing besides safety: `logrotate -d`
# prints a line per NAMED file, including "does not exist -- skipping", so a wrong path is visible
# in the dry run. A glob that matches nothing is silent and looks exactly like a healthy run.
#
# Widening any stanza to /var/log/msms/*.log on a host with LOG_DIR=/var/log/msms does all three of
# the following. None of them reports an error. One of them is permanent.
#
#   (a) RENAME OUT FROM UNDER AN OPEN DESCRIPTOR — a day of lines lost, silently.
#
#       The transport holds combined-<today>.log open for the whole day. logrotate's `create` mode
#       renames that file and puts a new empty one in its place, then expects the writer to be told
#       to reopen. Here there is nothing to tell: winston has no `reloadLogs` equivalent and
#       registers no SIGHUP handler — server.js:116-118 installs handlers for SIGTERM and SIGINT
#       and for nothing else. PM2's four files survive this treatment only because `pm2 reloadLogs`
#       exists, which is the whole reason stanza 1 has a postrotate at all.
#
#       The result is not an error message. It is a quiet split:
#
#       every line written for the rest of that day — including every morgan request line, which is
#       piped through winston at logger.js:61-65 — lands in the renamed file, while the file the
#       operator opens and greps stays empty. Both files exist, both look plausible, and the one
#       holding the evidence has a name nobody thinks to look at.
#
#   (b) PERMANENT DESTRUCTION OF RETENTION — the worst of the three, and the only irreversible one.
#
#       The hidden `.<sha1>-audit.json` files in winston's directory are its own ledger. Each holds
#       {"keep":{"days":true,"amount":30}} and then a list of every file the transport created, by
#       ABSOLUTE PATH plus a hash — and that hash is of the path and the creation timestamp, NOT of
#       the file's contents (node_modules/file-stream-rotator/FileStreamRotator.js:383 hashes
#       `logfile + "LOG_FILE" + time`). Pruning walks the list: FileStreamRotator.js:386-397 drops
#       every entry older than the window and calls removeFile(), which recomputes that hash at
#       :283 and then unlinks only `if (fs.existsSync(file.name))` at :285.
#
#       A file logrotate has renamed no longer exists under its recorded path, so the unlink is
#       skipped — and the entry is filtered out of the ledger in the same pass (:388-396), so it is
#       never reconsidered. That file is now permanently unowned: it sits there for ever while
#       LOG_RETENTION_DAYS still reads 30 and the mechanism still looks obeyed. Adding logrotate
#       here would break the half of row 7.10 that already works.
#
#   (c) TWO COMPRESSORS ON ONE DIRECTORY. Do not read this as "the directory is already fully
#       compressed" — it is not, and the live directory disproves it. `zippedArchive: true`
#       (logger.js:33, :40) compresses a day's file from the transport's `rotate` event
#       (node_modules/winston-daily-rotate-file/daily-rotate-file.js:128-175), which fires only
#       while the process is alive, and :140-142 skips the work when a `.gz` of that name already
#       exists. A process restarted across midnight therefore leaves that day uncompressed for
#       good. Counted in backend/storage/logs on 2026-09-04: FOURTEEN plain files (combined- and
#       error- for 2026-08-25..28 and 2026-09-02..04, the gaps being days the box was idle) against
#       exactly TWO `.gz` — combined-2026-09-03.log.gz and combined-2026-09-04.log.gz, both of
#       which also still have an uncompressed file of the same date. No error-*.log.gz exists at
#       all. A stanza here would meet that mixture, compress the plain files into a second archive
#       naming scheme, and leave two mechanisms fighting over one directory — on top of (a) and (b).
#
# If a size cap is ever wanted on the application logs, the fix belongs in
# backend/src/config/logger.js (a `maxSize` option on the two transports at :27-41), NOT here.
# deploy/monitoring/README.md:597-600 records that nothing bounds a single day's file today.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH PROCESSES PRODUCE THE FOUR FILES
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two apps, not three, despite "Queue Workers" appearing in §27's list of twelve. Both are declared
# in deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js, and both run ONE instance in fork mode.
#
# The instance count is not a capacity dial, it is a correctness setting, and it is also what makes
# the file count in INSTALL step 4 exactly four: in cluster mode PM2 suffixes each instance's log
# path with its pm_id, so `instances: 2` would silently turn four filenames into eight and half the
# stanzas below would stop matching anything.
#
# An earlier draft of this file described msms-api as "long-running, clustered", and
# ecosystem.config.js:48-52 still carries a live note about that contradiction. This is the line it
# points at: msms-api is NOT clustered, must not become so, and that note can now be retired.
#
#   msms-api    node src/server.js     `instances: 1, exec_mode: 'fork'` — ecosystem.config.js:317
#               names the app, :329-330 pin the mode. rateLimit.js:27-32 records that
#               express-rate-limit's store is the in-memory one — no `store:` is passed at
#               rateLimit.js:193-202 — so "behind N workers the effective ceiling is N × the
#               configured limit", and it names SRS §3 as describing a single-process deployment.
#               Read as a contract, "clustered" would have multiplied a security limit by the
#               instance count. The entitlement cache (a per-process Map, cache.js:32) and the
#               queue (queue.js:107) are per-process too; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md:233-237 agrees.
#
#   msms-cron   node src/jobs/cron.js  `instances: 1, exec_mode: 'fork'` — ecosystem.config.js:426,
#               :459-460. Two schedulers against one database double-notify and race renewals. It
#               refuses to start unless ENABLE_CRON=true (cron.js:249-255); env.js:257 defaults it
#               false and .env.example:116 ships it false, so ecosystem.config.js:501 sets it in
#               that app's own `env` block. See `missingok` in stanza 1 if that block is removed.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PATH CONTRACT — what the ecosystem file must keep doing for this file to work
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file and deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js are one mechanism split across two artifacts, and
# the contract runs in both directions.
#
# The ecosystem file MUST pin `out_file` and `error_file` for BOTH apps to the four absolute paths
# named at the top of this file (ecosystem.config.js:420-421, :530-531). If it stops doing so, PM2
# falls back to $PM2_HOME/logs/<app>-out.log, the stanzas below name files that never appear, and
# this file rotates nothing — quietly, because `missingok` makes an absent named file a non-event.
# This file MUST name those same paths and nothing wider (see the glob argument above). Neither
# half can detect the other drifting; INSTALL step 4 is the only thing that catches it, which is
# why reading that output is not optional.
#
# /var/log/msms is outside backend/storage/ on purpose: it keeps PM2's files out of the tree that
# holds uploads, backups and winston's own logs, so no glob written here can reach them. Create it:
#     mkdir -p /var/log/msms && chown msms:msms /var/log/msms && chmod 0750 /var/log/msms
#
# You do not have to take the four paths on trust either. `node` is the one tool this contract can
# be checked with, and the ecosystem file is a CommonJS module, so on the target host:
#
#   node -e "for (const a of require('/PATH/TO/deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js').apps) \
#            console.log(a.name, a.out_file, a.error_file)"
#
# prints the four absolute paths PM2 will actually open. They must be the four in the stanzas
# below. Run it AFTER the mkdir: ecosystem.config.js:227-235 deliberately THROWS when
# /var/log/msms is missing, and its message names this file.
#
# If ecosystem.config.js:213 is ever changed, the paths here are wrong and nothing announces it.
# Edit the two files in the same change.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CROSS-REFERENCES INTO THIS FILE — check them when you edit above this point
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three other deploy artifacts point back here. Two do so by line number, and line numbers move:
#
#   deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js:48-52, :190-201, :224, :230, :487 — several of these cite line
#     numbers in an EARLIER draft of this file (`:120`, `:137-149`, `:151-152`, `:153`, `:58-88`,
#     `:69-72`). The sections they describe all still exist here, but the numbers were written
#     against a draft that named different paths, so re-derive them before trusting one.
#   deploy/nginx/msms.conf:322-330 — states that this file deliberately does not rotate nginx's
#     logs. Still true; see the ownership block below.
#   deploy/mysql/production.cnf:1000-1005, :1029-1031 — states that this file covers only the PM2
#     stdout/stderr files in /var/log/msms. Stanza 3 also covers $PM2_HOME/pm2.log; nothing in that
#     document depends on the difference, but it is a small drift worth knowing about.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INSTALL — in this order; steps 1 and 5 are the ones that get skipped and then missed
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file ships with two placeholders. It is deliberately inert until both are substituted,
# because there is no correct literal to ship for either:
#
#   __PM2_HOME__   the PM2 daemon's home. The default is <deploy user's home>/.pm2, and
#                  `sudo -u msms pm2 report` prints the value actually in use.
#   __PM2_BIN__    the absolute path to the pm2 launcher. This is the single most likely thing in
#                  this file to be wrong on your host — see the postrotate comment in stanza 1.
#
# 1. Create the log directory and start PM2 FIRST, so the files exist before anything claims to
#    rotate them. PM2 opens out_file/error_file in order to spawn each process; logger.js:17
#    creates winston's directory only once Node is already running, which is too late to help here,
#    and ecosystem.config.js:227-235 refuses to load at all until this directory exists.
#
#      mkdir -p /var/log/msms && chown msms:msms /var/log/msms && chmod 0750 /var/log/msms
#      sudo -u msms pm2 start /PATH/TO/deploy/pm2/ecosystem.config.js
#      sudo -u msms pm2 save
#      ls -l /var/log/msms                            # expect the four files
#
#    On every host that is not the scheduler, start with `--only msms-api` instead
#    (ecosystem.config.js:479, :547): this ecosystem file carries ENABLE_CRON=true with it, and two
#    resident schedulers against one database double-notify and race renewals. Such a host then has
#    no msms-cron files at all, which is the case `missingok` exists for.
#
# 2. Substitute and install.
#
#      PM2_HOME_DIR=/home/msms/.pm2                   # confirm with: sudo -u msms pm2 report
#      PM2_BIN=$(sudo -u msms sh -lc 'command -v pm2')
#
#      install -o root -g root -m 0644 deploy/logrotate/msms /etc/logrotate.d/msms
#      sed -i "s#__PM2_HOME__#${PM2_HOME_DIR}#g; s#__PM2_BIN__#${PM2_BIN}#g" /etc/logrotate.d/msms
#
# 3. Prove the substitution completed. If either placeholder survives, the postrotate cannot run
#    and stanza 3 names a path that cannot exist — and both fail quietly.
#
#      if grep -q '__PM2_HOME__\|__PM2_BIN__' /etc/logrotate.d/msms; then \
#          echo 'SUBSTITUTION INCOMPLETE — this file will not reopen PM2 log descriptors'; fi
#
#    (Written as `grep -q` inside `if`, not as `n=$(grep -c ... || echo 0)`. That second idiom is
#    broken: `grep -c` prints 0 AND exits 1 when there are no matches, so the `||` fires and the
#    variable becomes the two-line string "0\n0", which then fails any arithmetic done on it. The
#    same warning is in deploy/mysql/production.cnf:1021-1023 for the same reason.)
#
# 4. Dry-run, and READ the output rather than checking the exit status. This changes nothing:
#
#      logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/msms
#
#    It must name all four PM2 app files plus __PM2_HOME__/pm2.log, and must NOT name any file
#    called error-<date>.log or combined-<date>.log. A stanza whose files are all missing is not an
#    error and does not change the exit status — reading the output IS the check.
#
#    On a host started with `--only msms-api` the expected count is two app files plus pm2.log, and
#    two "does not exist -- skipping" lines for the cron pair. Count what you expect, not four.
#
#    If this file is being skipped entirely it will be absent from:
#
#      logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf 2>&1 | grep msms
#
# 5. Force one real rotation, once, so a failing postrotate is discovered now and not on day 31:
#
#      logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/msms
#      ls -l /var/log/msms                                       # archives present
#      sudo -u msms pm2 logs msms-api --lines 5                  # and PM2 still writing
#
#    `dateext` refuses a second rotation on the same day ("destination already exists, skipping"),
#    so this is one shot per day. Do step 1 before step 5 or the forced run has nothing to rotate
#    and proves nothing at all.
#
# The installed name deliberately has NO extension. logrotate skips any file in /etc/logrotate.d
# whose suffix is in its taboo list — the default list includes `~`, `.rpmsave`, `.rpmorig`,
# `.rpmnew`, `.dpkg-old`, `.dpkg-dist`, `.dpkg-new`, `.dpkg-bak`, `.swp` and `.disabled`. Leaving an
# editor backup `msms~` beside this file is the classic way to end up with a rotation policy that is
# present, readable and never runs. (Plain `.bak` and `.conf` are NOT on the stock list, and an
# earlier draft of this comment claimed they were. Rather than trust either claim, the command in
# step 4 above is what settles it on the host.)
#
# The root:root 0644 mode matters for the same class of reason: logrotate refuses to read a config
# file that is writable by group or other, and reports that only in its own output — which on a
# healthy server nobody looks at until the disk is full.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHO OWNS WHICH FILE — read this before adding a stanza
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Six log-producing things on this box; logrotate is responsible for two of them. Getting the
# boundary wrong does not merely duplicate work — in case 1 it actively breaks something that works,
# and in case 4 it stops nginx's rotation dead for the whole run.
#
#   1. winston application logs      OWNED BY THE APP.        logrotate must not touch them.
#      backend/storage/logs/error-YYYY-MM-DD.log         (logger.js:30-33)
#      backend/storage/logs/combined-YYYY-MM-DD.log      (logger.js:37-40)
#      backend/storage/logs/cron/…                       the scheduler's own pair; the separate
#                                                        directory comes from ecosystem.config.js:502
#      backend/storage/logs/*.log.gz  and  .<sha1>-audit.json
#      Rotated by date and pruned by age by the transports themselves. See the three failures above.
#
#   2. PM2's per-app stdout/stderr   OWNED BY THIS FILE.      Stanzas 1 and 2.
#      The four files in /var/log/msms. PM2 never rotates or bounds these itself.
#
#   3. PM2's own daemon log          OWNED BY THIS FILE.      Stanza 3.
#      __PM2_HOME__/pm2.log — PM2's supervision record (app started, app exited with code N,
#      restart counters). It is NOT one of the four files in item 2: those receive only what the
#      supervised process itself writes to stdout/stderr. The daemon log sits directly in
#      $PM2_HOME, not in $PM2_HOME/logs, so it is easy to leave out, and it is the one file in this
#      deployment with no retention of any kind behind it.
#
#   4. nginx access/error logs       OWNED BY THE DISTRO.     /etc/logrotate.d/nginx ships with the
#      nginx package and already performs the correct `kill -USR1` reopen. A second stanza naming
#      the same paths makes logrotate abort that run with "duplicate log entry for
#      /var/log/nginx/access.log" and skip rotation for everything after it. Do not add one here.
#      deploy/nginx/msms.conf:322-330 states the same boundary from the other side.
#
#   5. MySQL error and slow logs     OWNED BY THE DISTRO — in theory.
#      deploy/mysql/production.cnf:1011-1031 records that nothing in this repository rotates them,
#      that the packaged mysql-server logrotate entry is assumed to, and that the assumption is
#      unverified. If it turns out nothing covers them, the stanza belongs in that packaged entry,
#      NOT here — for the duplicate-log-entry reason in item 4.
#
#   6. mysqldump backups             OWNED BY THE APP.        Not logs, and self-pruning — but not
#      in the general way an earlier draft of this comment claimed.
#      backend/src/jobs/tasks/databaseBackup.js:127-139 deletes ONLY files whose name matches its
#      own output — the filter at :132 requires the `<db-name>-` prefix and the `.sql` suffix — and
#      older than BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS by mtime. prune() is called at :178, i.e. only AFTER a dump
#      has succeeded and passed the non-empty guard at :173-176, so a failed or zero-byte dump
#      prunes nothing that night. The directory does not self-clean in general; it self-cleans its
#      own dumps on its own successful runs. No stanza here could reach them anyway, but the
#      reasoning is recorded so nobody adds one "for tidiness".
#
#   `npm run worker` and `npm run db:backup` produce no file for anyone to rotate: worker.js runs
#   one job in its own process and exits (worker.js:17-22), and both write to the operator's
#   terminal rather than to a PM2 log. There is NO resident queue worker to supervise — a separate
#   process cannot see the API's in-memory queue (queue.js:107 is one MemoryQueue per process), and
#   §29's 64-table ceiling forbids the jobs table that would be needed;
#   docs/ARCHITECTURE.md:239-242 says the same. The nightly backup is scheduled inside msms-cron
#   (databaseBackup.js:193, `0 3 * * *`), so no system crontab entry is involved either.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS THEN LEFT FOR LOGROTATE, AND WHY IT IS NOT NOTHING
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In production winston does not write to the console at all — the Console transport is added only
# when the environment is neither production nor test (logger.js:44), and ecosystem.config.js:349
# sets NODE_ENV=production for msms-api (:500 does the same for msms-cron). So neither app's own
# log lines reach PM2's out/err files, and one could conclude those files are empty and
# unimportant.
#
# They are not, and the reason is specific. What lands there is exactly what winston cannot capture,
# which is to say what you need when the process will not run:
#
#   * A failure during module load, BEFORE any handler exists. server.js:27 requires
#     ./config/logger at module scope, and logger.js:17 calls
#     `fs.mkdirSync(config.logging.dir, { recursive: true })` with no try/catch. If the log
#     directory cannot be created — wrong owner after a deploy, a full disk, a bad LOG_DIR — that
#     throws out of `require`, and Node prints an uncaught EACCES/ENOSPC stack to stderr. The
#     `Failed to start:` line does NOT appear in this case: `start().catch(...)` is not installed
#     until server.js:141. An earlier draft of this comment credited that line with covering exactly
#     the one failure it cannot cover.
#   * Failures raised INSIDE start(), where the logger did load — assertRuntimeConfig()
#     (env.js:265), assertConnection(), initCache(). Those reach server.js:141-148, which writes
#     `Failed to start: <message>` to stderr at :147 as well as to the log, "because a configuration
#     error can be thrown before the log directory is usable". That line is written once per
#     crash-loop iteration, and it is the reason `copytruncate` is refused in stanzas 1 and 2.
#   * EADDRINUSE and other listen errors (server.js:106-114), V8 and libuv fatal errors (heap OOM,
#     native module load failure), Node deprecation and process warnings, and any stack trace
#     printed after the runtime has given up. None of these goes through a logger.
#   * The cron process's refusal to start, which is a bare `console.error` (cron.js:250-254).
#
# A crash-looping API therefore produces a growing PM2 error log and an application log that says
# nothing at all. Rotating these files is the difference between having that evidence and having a
# full /var — and deploy/monitoring/README.md:315-323 tells the operator to read `pm2 logs` for
# exactly these cases, which only works while the files are still there.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
