# ==================================================================================================


# ==================================================================================================
# STANZA 1 — the two ERROR logs. Rotated unconditionally, on purpose.
# ==================================================================================================
/var/log/msms/msms-api-error.log
/var/log/msms/msms-cron-error.log
{

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # NO notifempty HERE, and this is the difference between a policy that can fail once and a
    # policy that can fail permanently.
    #
    # logrotate renames the file BEFORE running postrotate. If `pm2 reloadLogs` then fails, PM2
    # keeps its descriptor on the renamed inode and the newly created msms-api-error.log stays 0
    # bytes for ever. With `notifempty`, that empty file would then be skipped every subsequent
    # night — and because `sharedscripts` runs postrotate only when at least one file in the set
    # actually rotated, the reopen would never be retried. One transient failure becomes permanent,
    # silent log loss, with `delaycompress` gzipping a file PM2 is still appending to and, after
    # `rotate` archives, the still-open inode unlinked while the descriptor holds the disk space.
    #
    # Without notifempty the set rotates every night regardless, so postrotate is attempted every
    # night and a failure keeps announcing itself instead of latching. The cost is up to `rotate`
    # near-empty archives on a healthy, quiet server. That is a trivial price for keeping the retry
    # alive, and it is the whole reason this stanza is split out from the -out.log files.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # su — what it actually buys, which is NOT what an earlier draft claimed.
    #
    # It is not the insecure-parent-directory check. logrotate refuses to rotate inside a directory
    # that is group- or world-WRITABLE; INSTALL step 1 makes /var/log/msms mode 0750 owned
    # msms:msms, which is neither, so that check never fires — and an operator who tests "is su
    # needed?" will find rotation works without it and be tempted to delete the line.
    #
    # What `su` does is switch uid/gid before the rotation and before the postrotate script. Two
    # things depend on that. The archives and the replacement files are created as `msms` rather
    # than root, in a directory root cannot be assumed to keep writable for the app. And
    # `pm2 reloadLogs` runs as the account that owns the daemon: run as root with PM2_HOME pointed
    # at the msms daemon, pm2 may reach the socket, but if that daemon is not running it will spawn
    # a NEW one and write root-owned files into __PM2_HOME__ — after which the real daemon cannot
    # write its own directory and the situation needs a human.
    #
    # (It also covers the insecure-permissions refusal if a deploy ever leaves the directory
    # group-writable. That is a second reason, not the reason.)
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    su msms msms

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # daily + rotate 30 + maxage 30 — deliberately the same window as LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30
    # (.env.example:108, env.js:245, deploy/env/production.env.example:672).
    #
    # This number is a mirror of the application's retention, not a convention. An incident is
    # investigated from both halves at once: the winston combined log says what the request did, the
    # PM2 error log says whether the process survived it. If one window is shorter, an investigation
    # that reaches back far enough finds half the story and no indication the other half was ever
    # there.
    #
    # THEY MUST BE CHANGED TOGETHER. If LOG_RETENTION_DAYS is raised in .env, raise both numbers
    # here in the same change.
    #
    # `maxage` is present because `rotate` alone is a COUNT, and a count only advances when a
    # rotation happens, whereas winston prunes by AGE. Pairing the two makes the two halves age out
    # together instead of drifting apart whenever rotations are skipped. Be honest about its limit:
    # maxage is also applied only while logrotate is processing this log set, so it is a backstop
    # for irregular rotation, not for a set that has stopped being processed entirely — which is the
    # failure `notifempty` could cause, and which is why it is not used in this stanza.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    daily
    rotate 30
    maxage 30

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # dateext + dateyesterday — name archives by the date of their CONTENTS.
    #
    # dateext alone gets this wrong in a way that looks right. logrotate runs from cron.daily or
    # logrotate.timer in the early morning, and with plain `dateext` the suffix is the date of the
    # RUN — so Tuesday's PM2 lines land in msms-api-error.log-<Wednesday>, while Tuesday's
    # application lines are in combined-<Tuesday>.log (logger.js:37-38). An operator correlating the
    # two halves during an incident then opens the wrong archive by exactly one day, and the two
    # naming schemes are similar enough that nothing signals the mistake.
    #
    # `dateyesterday` uses the previous day's date for the suffix, which is the date of the content
    # being rotated, so both halves answer "which file holds Tuesday" the same way. It needs
    # logrotate 3.9.0+ — see the verification block in the header before installing.
    #
    # The default numbered suffixes are rejected for a different reason: every rotation renames every
    # archive, so a file's name depends on when you looked at it — bad when someone is copying
    # filenames into an incident note.
    #
    # Caveat, since it is a real one: with dateext a second rotation on the same day cannot happen
    # ("destination already exists, skipping rotation"). That is correct here because the only
    # trigger is the distribution's once-a-day run, but it is why `maxsize` is not used, and it is
    # why INSTALL step 5 is a one-shot.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    dateext
    dateformat -%Y-%m-%d
    dateyesterday

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # missingok — and a precise statement of what it does and does not cover.
    #
    # It covers a legitimately absent file. Every host except the scheduler is started with
    # `pm2 start ... --only msms-api` (ecosystem.config.js:479, :547), so msms-cron-error.log does
    # not exist there at all; erroring by mail every night about that teaches the operator to filter
    # logrotate's mail, after which the next real error goes with it.
    #
    # It does NOT cover a wrong path, and that is the cost of the directive: a path that no longer
    # matches ecosystem.config.js:213 is also "missing", so it too is silent. INSTALL step 3 (the
    # placeholder check) and step 4 (reading the -d output) are what distinguish the two cases.
    # Nothing else will.
    #
    # One case it is NOT protecting against, despite an earlier draft saying so: a deployment with
    # ENABLE_CRON unset does not simply have no cron log. If msms-cron is started while ENABLE_CRON
    # is not true, cron.js:249-255 prints its refusal and exits 1 on EVERY start, so this file is
    # present and filling with restart banners rather than absent. The shipped ecosystem file sets
    # ENABLE_CRON in that app's own env block (ecosystem.config.js:501) precisely so this does not
    # happen; if someone removes it, that is what a non-empty, fast-growing msms-cron-error.log
    # means. It is bounded, not infinite — ecosystem.config.js:277 backs off between restarts and
    # :310 caps at `max_restarts: 20`, after which PM2 leaves the app `errored` — but 20 refusals
    # with no sweeps running is still the loudest thing this log will ever say.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    missingok

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # compress + delaycompress.
    #
    # delaycompress is not cosmetic here. The reopen below happens in postrotate, i.e. after
    # logrotate has already renamed the file; if compression ran in the same pass, gzip could be
    # reading the rotated inode while PM2 is still appending to it, producing a truncated or garbled
    # tail of exactly the crash trace that prompted the investigation. delaycompress defers
    # compression to the next run, by which time the reopen is a day old.
    #
    # (These four files are never pre-compressed — PM2 writes them plain — so unlike winston's
    # directory there is no second compressor here to collide with.)
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    compress
    delaycompress

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # create, NOT copytruncate — and this is the choice that loses log lines if made wrong.
    #
    # The rule is whether the writing process can be told to reopen its file. PM2 can:
    # `pm2 reloadLogs` closes and reopens the descriptors it holds for its managed apps. So the
    # correct pattern is rename-then-reopen, which is lossless — the daemon writes to the old inode
    # until it is told, then switches cleanly to the new file.
    #
    # copytruncate would be wrong here in two distinct ways, both silent:
    #   * Every line written between the copy and the truncate is destroyed. The line that matters
    #     most in this file — server.js:147's "Failed to start: <message>" — is written exactly once
    #     per boot attempt. Losing it means the operator sees an empty error log for an API that is
    #     crash-looping, and concludes the process is fine.
    #   * A descriptor opened without O_APPEND keeps its byte offset across the truncate. The next
    #     write lands at the old offset and the kernel fills the gap with NUL bytes, giving a sparse
    #     file that reports hundreds of megabytes, opens as binary in most tools, and cannot be read
    #     from the top.
    #
    # The mode and ownership are load-bearing, not decoration: if root creates the replacement file,
    # the PM2 process reopening it as `msms` gets EACCES, drops the stream, and logs nothing further
    # until someone restarts it. 0640 keeps stack traces and configuration errors off a
    # world-readable path, which matters more in /var/log than it would inside the app tree, because
    # /var/log is where an operator's habits say everything is readable.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    create 0640 msms msms

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # sharedscripts — `pm2 reloadLogs` is global, so it runs once for this stanza's files rather
    # than once per file. Without it the daemon is asked to reopen everything twice here, and each
    # extra call is another window in which a write can land on a descriptor being swapped.
    #
    # Note that stanza 2 carries the same postrotate. Two reloads on a night when both stanzas
    # rotate is harmless — each one reopens all four descriptors — and it is what makes stanza 2's
    # `notifempty` safe: even if an out-log stanza latches empty, this stanza's nightly reload
    # reopens its descriptors anyway. THE TWO POSTROTATE BLOCKS MUST BE KEPT IDENTICAL.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    sharedscripts
    postrotate
        # PATH is set explicitly because logrotate's scripts run with a minimal environment, and
        # pm2's launcher is a `#!/usr/bin/env node` script — so a PATH without node's directory
        # fails with `env: node: No such file or directory` and exit 127, which looks like a pm2
        # problem and is not.
        #
        # THIS IS THE LINE MOST LIKELY TO BE WRONG ON YOUR HOST, which is why the binary is a
        # placeholder rather than a guessed literal such as /usr/bin/pm2. On the target machine run,
        # as the deploy user:
        #     command -v pm2 ; command -v node
        # __PM2_BIN__ is the first of those. If node is NOT in the PATH below — under nvm both live
        # in ~/.nvm/versions/node/<ver>/bin — prepend `dirname $(command -v node)` to the PATH line
        # here, in this stanza and in stanza 2. A system PATH will not find an nvm node.
        #
        # PM2 is per-user: the daemon holding the descriptors belongs to `msms`, and a pm2 invoked
        # with a different PM2_HOME talks to a different (or newly spawned) daemon and reopens
        # nothing while exiting 0. Both the absolute binary path and PM2_HOME are required because
        # these scripts run without a guaranteed HOME under `su`.
        #
        # stdout is discarded; stderr is deliberately NOT, and there is no `|| true`. If this fails,
        # the descriptors were never reopened, and the operator must see it.
        PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
        export PATH
        PM2_HOME=__PM2_HOME__ __PM2_BIN__ reloadLogs >/dev/null
    endscript
}


# ==================================================================================================
# STANZA 2 — the two OUT logs. Identical to stanza 1 except for `notifempty`.
# ==================================================================================================
# That one word is the whole difference between the stanzas, and it is deliberate in both
# directions. Added in session 26: the asymmetry was here before, correct, and unexplained.
#
# `notifempty` skips the rotation when the file is empty. Both kinds of file are empty most days in
# production — `logger.js:44` drops the console transport under NODE_ENV=production, so nothing
# routine is written to either — but an empty file means opposite things in the two cases:
#
#   - An empty OUT log is the normal, uninformative state. Rotating it produces a dated, compressed,
#     empty file that records nothing anybody will ever read, thirty times a month. `notifempty` is
#     right here.
#
#   - An empty ERROR log is a FINDING. These files hold exactly what winston cannot: a syntax error
#     or missing module that kills the process before `require('./config/logger')` returns, an
#     EADDRINUSE racing the logger, cron's refusal (`cron.js:250-254`), and the deliberate
#     `process.stderr.write` on the boot-failure path (`server.js:147`). Rotating unconditionally
#     means every day has a file: `msms-api-error.log-2026-09-04` existing and empty says "nothing
#     failed that day", while its absence would be ambiguous between "nothing failed" and "logrotate
#     never ran". For the one file that proves a process could not start, being able to tell those
#     two apart is worth thirty empty files a month.
# ==================================================================================================
/var/log/msms/msms-api-out.log
/var/log/msms/msms-cron-out.log
{
    su msms msms

    daily
    rotate 30
    maxage 30

    dateext
    dateformat -%Y-%m-%d
    dateyesterday

    missingok

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # notifempty — in production these two files are empty BY DESIGN.
    #
    # winston adds its Console transport only outside production (logger.js:44) and
    # ecosystem.config.js:349 sets NODE_ENV=production for msms-api (:500 for msms-cron), so a
    # healthy pair writes nothing at all to stdout. Without this, both files rotate every night and
    # 30 zero-byte archives accumulate beside the error archives that actually matter, burying them.
    #
    # It is safe HERE and not in stanza 1 for one reason: if a postrotate failure ever left these
    # files empty and latched, stanza 1 still rotates nightly and its postrotate reopens ALL of
    # PM2's descriptors, including these two. The out logs recover on stanza 1's back. The error
    # logs have nothing behind them, which is why they carry the cost of rotating empty instead.
    #
    # Consequence worth stating: because rotations here are skipped on quiet days, `rotate 30` for
    # these two files can span considerably more than 30 days. `maxage 30` above is what bounds
    # that, and it is the reason it appears in this stanza too.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    notifempty

    compress
    delaycompress

    create 0640 msms msms

    sharedscripts
    postrotate
        # KEEP IDENTICAL TO STANZA 1. See the long comment there for why each part is present and
        # for what to check on the target host (`command -v pm2`, `command -v node`, nvm paths).
        PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
        export PATH
        PM2_HOME=__PM2_HOME__ __PM2_BIN__ reloadLogs >/dev/null
    endscript
}


# ==================================================================================================
# STANZA 3 — PM2's own daemon log. Different file, different mechanism, deliberately separate.
# ==================================================================================================
# __PM2_HOME__/pm2.log is written by the PM2 daemon itself, not by a supervised app. It holds the
# supervision record — app started, app exited with code N, restart counters climbing — which is
# precisely the evidence for a crash loop, and it is the only file in this deployment with no
# retention behind it at all: nothing in the application prunes it, and it is not in
# $PM2_HOME/logs, so a glob written for the app logs cannot reach it. Left alone it grows until the
# partition fills — and that partition is the deploy user's home, not /var.
# ==================================================================================================
__PM2_HOME__/pm2.log
{
    # Same reasoning as stanza 1: the archive and the truncation should happen as the account that
    # owns $PM2_HOME, not as root.
    su msms msms

    daily
    rotate 30
    maxage 30

    dateext
    dateformat -%Y-%m-%d
    dateyesterday

    # A host where PM2 has never been started has no pm2.log. Same caveat as stanza 1: this makes a
    # mis-substituted __PM2_HOME__ silent too, which is what INSTALL steps 3 and 4 are for.
    missingok
    notifempty

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # copytruncate here, and `create` deliberately absent — the opposite choice from stanzas 1 and
    # 2, for the reason stated there: it depends entirely on whether the writer can be told to
    # reopen.
    #
    # `pm2 reloadLogs` reopens the descriptors PM2 holds for its managed APPS. There is no
    # documented command that makes the daemon reopen its OWN log, so rename-then-reopen would
    # leave the daemon writing to a renamed inode for ever — the exact failure stanza 1's postrotate
    # exists to prevent, and the same failure as (a) in the header.
    #
    # The known cost is real and is accepted knowingly: any line written between the copy and the
    # truncate is lost. For a supervision log that writes a handful of lines per restart, losing a
    # line in that window is a far smaller loss than losing the whole file to a stale descriptor.
    #
    # THE ONE THING TO CHECK AFTER THE FIRST ROTATION. copytruncate is only safe if the writer holds
    # the file with O_APPEND; without it the daemon keeps its old offset and the truncated file is
    # refilled with NUL bytes up to that offset. pm2 is not installed on the machine this file was
    # written on and is not a dependency of this project, so its open flags could NOT be read from
    # source. Verify on the host after the first rotation:
    #     head -c 64 __PM2_HOME__/pm2.log | od -c | head
    # Leading \0 bytes mean copytruncate is wrong for this PM2 build. `rotate 0` plus `maxsize` is
    # NOT the answer either — instead stop rotating this file here, and restart the daemon
    # (`pm2 update`, which re-execs it and reopens pm2.log) on a schedule the operator chooses, and
    # record why in this comment.
    #
    # No delaycompress: with copytruncate the archive is a completed copy, not the live inode, so
    # nothing is still appending to it and there is no reason to wait a day to compress it.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    copytruncate
    compress

    # No postrotate. Nothing needs to be told anything: the descriptor was never invalidated.
}
